**Chapter 10-22: ‘Preparatory Measures and Refusal’**

——This occurred at the same time that Natsuki Subaru, in the ‘Six Tongues’’ hideout, was being informed of the colossal calamity afflicting the Kingdom of Lugunica, in a corner of the royal capital’s commercial district.

“—By some means or other, the enemy has already laid their hands on the Dragon History Stone and seized full authority. That is the situation the Royal Castle currently finds itself in. I’ve filled in the gaps in our information with my own speculation, but I doubt I’m far off the mark.”

“…Indeed. It also explains why we’re being hunted at the moment. At the very least, we should consider the chain of command at the Royal Castle to be under their control.”

“——”

“Garfiel, are you alright?”

“Huh? Y-yeah, I’m fine… Nah, honestly, talkin’ about a scale I never even imagined… I just dunno what to say.”

Shaking his head in denial, Garfiel confessed his feelings with complete honesty. But Otto could sympathize with his younger brother-in-arms; it was only natural.

“…Given the situation, after all.”

Frankly speaking, Otto was equally plagued by chaos and bewilderment. The only difference was their roles within the camp—Otto was not permitted the luxury of simply stopping his thoughts.

In this cornered situation, Garfiel had fulfilled his duty admirably. —No, not just Garfiel. It was the result of everyone banding together and giving their all. The only one who had failed was himself, shamefully disarmed as he was.

“—Tch.”

Leaning his back against the room’s wall, sitting while clutching one knee, Garfiel groaned in pain.

The intense exhaustion and pain not fading from his face was something unthinkable for Garfiel, who possessed absurd toughness and regenerative power. That alone spoke volumes about the force behind the blow Garfiel had received—the power possessed by Reinhard, something beyond all norms and utterly unreasonable.

—Some time had passed since Reinhard had blocked Otto’s group’s path, and they had barely managed to break through using every single card they had.

The battle hadn’t lasted even a single minute, but by its end, everyone was battered and bruised. Garfiel, who had confronted Reinhard head-on, was in a particularly terrible state. While he had deliberately lured the opponent into carelessness, he felt pathetic for being rendered immobile just by having his chin grazed.

The victory they’d somehow snatched had been thanks to unexpected reinforcements, and including being driven into that situation in the first place, Otto had a great deal to reflect upon.

The fact that they could even hold something like a reflection meeting was only because the reinforcements they’d joined up with had led them to secure this temporary safe zone.

“—. Just managing to shake him off is an impressive feat. Even if his role was forced upon him, a hundred ordinary soldiers wouldn’t even lay a finger on him.”

“But for that, we were forced to play a card that should never have been played. She’s cast aside something immense from her own life. I cannot forgive that person.”

“…Your indignation is likely justified. He’s surely prepared to be resented, too. That’s what it means to be the ‘Sword Saint.’ Even I have never lost my anger towards the ‘Sword Saint.’”

“That’s…”

Otto narrowed his eyes quietly at the person who placed a large hand on his own bald head.

What he’d been about to continue—whether a question or a guess—he swallowed his words because he realized he’d already spoken of something that shouldn’t be said.

But—

“—That anger is directed not at Reinhard, but at my wife, I presume.”

Instead of Otto, who had fallen silent, the old swordsman who opened the entrance door—Wilhelm—took the words from him with a tone of conviction.

Wilhelm, entering the room, was accompanied by Rem and a short-haired elderly man. After ushering Rem inside first, he turned to the man,

“Conwood, my apologies for the trouble. I am in your debt for sheltering us.”

“Hah, to be earnestly thanked by the once so overbearing and willful Wilhelm Trias—maybe it’ll rain tomorrow.”

“You…”

“It’s a joke. —Besides, it’s only natural. It’s only natural, Wilhelm. Me lending you a hand is something that doesn’t even warrant thanks. It’s simply natural.”

“——”

Wilhelm’s cheeks stiffened at the man’s trembling voice after those casual words.

The man relaxed his cheeks as if amused by his old acquaintance’s reaction, then looked at Otto, Garfiel, and the others inside the room,

“I owe a debt to your comrade, Natsuki Subaru-dono, from the time of the White Whale subjugation. A debt so large it’d take a lifetime to repay. I won’t let anyone through here, so rest easy.”

“…Shopkeeper, you have our gratitude.”

“I told you. Our gratitude is far greater. …No one believes Natsuki Subaru-dono is part of the Witch’s Cult. The same goes for Crusch-sama.”

Saying that, the man clapped Wilhelm on the shoulder and exited, closing the door. Watching his back disappear behind the door, Otto called out, “Rem-san.”

This place, reached under Wilhelm’s guidance, was a private room in a tavern offered as a shelter. While Otto and the others explained the situation in this room, Rem had been in a separate room.

The reason for that—

“Are Petra-chan and the others alright?”

“…For the time being, they’re resting. Petra-chan was quite distraught, and Meili-chan was the one soothing her… but both of them have fallen asleep.”

“I see… Understood. Thank you.”

“No, I did nothing. More importantly, the discussion?”

“—We’ve just finished confirming the current situation, I suppose.”

Otto answered Rem, whose expression was tense, directing his light-blue eyes toward the back of the room.

Following her gaze, there stood—or rather, sat—a large figure on a seat in the private room, whose head was still higher than Otto or Wilhelm’s. A bald head, sun-tanned skin, an elderly giant—his name was—

“—Barga Cromwell. One of the primary figures who escalated the kingdom’s civil war in the past.”

“…Unfortunately, I’ve no idea who you might be talking about. I’m just Old Man Rom, a decrepit old giant living in the slums, nothing more.”

“How brazen. What of the scar on your chest that I carved? Are you hiding it under that magnificent white chest hair of yours?”

“I understand even less. It’s not about me, but one truly hates aging. Even the renowned ‘Sword Devil’ can’t compete with the mental decline of old age, it seems.”

“You bastard—”

“…Both of you, please stop fighting. Petra-chan and the others are resting upstairs. Don’t we have no time for internal squabbling?”

Stepping forward as if to wedge herself between the two glaring men, Rem glared at them in turn. Under her stern gaze, Wilhelm lowered his head, and the giant figure—the old giant calling himself Old Man Rom—also cleared his throat, showing a willingness to follow Rem’s lead.

While grateful for Rem’s intervention, Otto couldn’t help but feel a certain sense of understanding regarding the hostile exchange between the two.

—Otto himself was a citizen of this Dragonfriend Kingdom of Lugunica.
He knew the fact that ‘Sword Devil’ Wilhelm van Astrea was a hero of the ‘Demi-Human War,’ and that Barga Cromwell was one of the commanders of the Demi-Human Alliance during that civil war.

He never imagined Barga Cromwell was still alive.

Of course, Old Man Rom himself denied any connection to Barga and would likely never admit it. In any case—

“I will call you Rom-san… We’re grateful you sent Wilhelm-san as reinforcements. Without that, we would have been annihilated by now.”

“That’s no exaggeration. If they’d seized us in that state and taken us to the castle, anyone who isn’t a royal selection candidate or a knight could easily be eliminated and covered up.”

“Eliminated and covered up, that’s…”

“We must assume the worst-case scenario. Shrinking from it and clinging to optimism won’t make the enemy show any mercy. …Especially now that Miklotov is dead.”

Rem was left speechless by Old Man Rom’s grim assessment of the situation, but Otto agreed. Always assume the worst possibility. Ultimately, that’s what leads to finding a way out of a desperate situation. Pessimistic, he thought to himself.

“Yo, Wilhelm-san, you saved my ass earlier.”

At that, Garfiel, sitting by the wall, raised a hand lightly towards Wilhelm. At his call, Wilhelm turned, “Garfiel-dono,”

“At that moment, my skill was insufficient; I apologize. I couldn’t land a blade on Reinhard without the surprise tactic of cutting you as well.”

“I was prepared for that when I took it, so don’t sweat it. This wound’s already almost closed up. …The ‘Sword Saint’’s hand chop though, that’s a whole ’nother story.”

“——”

“More ’mportantly, what’ve you been doin’, Wilhelm-san? I heard our boss and that Duchess were escapin’ together…”

“Indeed. Allow me to recount what happened on my end.”

So saying, Wilhelm solemnly began to explain what had happened to him, and to his master Crusch, up until now.

“After Subaru-dono and Emilia-sama visited the mansion and saw everyone off, Crusch-sama shut herself in her room. Naturally, neither I nor any of the household staff could approach her, and she barely took any meals.”

“That… must have been worrying.”

“Yes. Everyone knew things couldn’t go on like that. So, I sought the assistance of Crusch-sama’s father, the former Duke, Meckart Karsten-sama, and left the mansion to make the necessary arrangements. However, during that time—”

“—Crusch-sama left the mansion alone and headed for the residence of Lord Miklotov McMahon.”

“Precisely. By the time word reached my ears, it was already too late.”

With an expression that couldn’t hide his regret, Wilhelm lamented his own inadequacy.

But if Crusch had deliberately chosen a moment when Wilhelm was absent, it would only be a matter of sooner or later, and there was no reason to blame him. Though, given this outcome, the ‘Sword Devil’’s self-reproach was understandable.

“Naturally, it was unthinkable that Crusch-sama would commit such a reckless act. I immediately went up to the Royal Castle and appealed to an old acquaintance, Lord Bordeaux Zellgef. However, not only was I not listened to, but on my way back, I ended up being pursued by soldiers. Then—”

“—I called out to him. Frankly, it was a bitter choice.”

“…I, too, had no desire to forcibly cut down kingdom soldiers and break through. When I saw this man in the light, I couldn’t believe my eyes.”

“Both of you.”

Ire and a grudge that couldn’t be erased inevitably poisoned their words.

As Rem lightly scolded the two men, Otto understood the circumstances of their meeting. And the circumstances leading up to Wilhelm joining them.

“So, what about your side, Rom-san? You seem remarkably well-prepared, as if you made your move quite early.”

“It’s natural to be suspicious, but I only moved just before, much the same as you lot. I noticed because my nose is sharp… I’ve always had a good intuition for approaching danger. It had gotten a bit rusty, but the recent turmoil seems to have scoured the rust off.”

“I see, is that so? —Understood.”

Rolling his large shoulders, Old Man Rom answered plainly, and Otto refrained from pressing further.

They were only facing the same direction at this moment; fundamentally, their positions were opposed factions. It was natural to keep cards hidden, and faulting that would be narrow-minded.

At the very least, Old Man Rom’s actions had saved Otto and the others from a pinch. For now, he should prioritize evaluating that action over suspecting his true intentions.

“What a scary young lad. Your eyes aren’t smiling at all.”

“My apologies. A merchant’s basic rule is to smile outwardly while smirking inwardly, but lately, I haven’t been able to act very merchant-like. I disgust even myself. —Though, if being a good-natured merchant means getting one’s comrades killed, then such a person is unnecessary right now.”

“——”

“…My apologies, I spoke out of turn.”

After responding with a shrug, Otto admonished himself; those were unnecessary words.

Just thinking them is one thing, but voicing them makes them akin to a weapon. If you suddenly draw a weapon during a meeting, you can’t complain about being stabbed to death.

Evidently, being surpassed in his ‘Divine Protection of Word Spirits’ was affecting him more than he realized. He’d warned himself, almost like a curse, that entrusting his identity to his Divine Protection was dangerous, yet he’d relied on it too much recently. This was the price.

“—In any case, we all share the same position here. Our lords have been unjustly slandered, and the Royal Castle is condoning it… no, actively leading it. Their goal is to eliminate the royal selection candidates, destroy the very rules of the royal selection, and dominate the kingdom.”

“And for that, Emilia-san and that person are being maligned… The ones Wilhelm-san and Rom-san support are in the same situation, aren’t they?”

“Emilia-sama, Felt-sama, the Duchess… so the only ones left are Anastasia-sama and the church’s new candidate, Filore-sama.”

“Who knows? If the ‘Sage Council’ or the participants of the half-roundtable are unscrupulous, there are countless ways to slander Anastasia-sama. She’s from Kararagi, so they could claim she’s arranging for the city-state to invade the kingdom… Or, they could say she conspired with Emilia-sama in the Vollachia Empire to murder Priscilla-sama—”

“—Ngh, don’t you dare joke about that!”

At Otto’s prediction of a vicious scenario, Rem raised her voice in anger.

Her light-blue eyes moist, breathing heavily, she clenched her fists tightly, her fury directed at the slanderous speculation regarding Priscilla’s death that Otto had hypothesized.

“Slandering and exploiting Priscilla-san’s death… I absolutely won’t allow it. If anyone seriously suggests such a thing, I won’t forgive them…! If word of this reaches Abel-san, it will mean war with Vollachia!”

“Indeed, it’s no laughing matter. Treating Emilia-sama coldly, when she brought back achievements that could lead to a friendship treaty with the Empire, is equivalent to slinging mud at the face of the Empire that acknowledged her. It’s an absurd reason for war, but history is full of absurd reasons for war. And besides…”

“—Even if it came to war, Reinhard is there. Controlling the Royal Castle means being able to move the ‘Sword Saint.’ Previously, the authority to deploy the ‘Sword Saint’ was strictly managed to prevent that… but the enemy has gotten hold of it.”

“Something like, that…”

It was only the worst-case scenario, but Old Man Rom didn’t deny Otto’s thoughts; instead, he paved the nightmare by filling in the missing parts.

The sheer horror of it left Rem and Garfiel, who had just barely escaped a life-or-death bout with Reinhard, speechless.

Only one person—

“—The ‘Divine Protection of the Sword Saint,’ is it.”

The ‘Sword Devil’ merely murmured at a volume almost no one could hear.

“…Judging by the situation, the enemy clearly intends to attack the royal selection. That being the case, we must assume Filore-sama, who recently stepped onto the stage, and the ‘Dragon God Church’ supporting her, are on the enemy’s side. Just to confirm, regarding the princess who vanished fifteen years ago…”

“—Felt is Felt. There’s no other answer.”

Hating the stagnation silence brought to their discussion, Otto tried to organize the situation and confirm facts, but Old Man Rom’s words cut him off hard and clear.

Realizing that a denial overlapping a question wasn’t kind enough, Old Man Rom added “But,” to the surrounding gazes,

“I can only consider the story of the ‘Dragon God Church’ sheltering the lost princess as dubious. Even if they kept their distance from the castle, staying silent about it for over a year after the royal selection began is unthinkable during a crisis threatening the kingdom’s very existence.”

Old Man Rom, balancing a firm opinion with an uncertain attitude, was clearly hiding something.

Naturally, the ‘Sword Devil’’s gaze directed at the old giant grew sharper.

“——”

It’s said that Wilhelm, who was the captain of the Royal Guard at the time fifteen years ago, was responsible for the search for the lost princess. His desire to know the truth behind the princess rumors hanging over Felt and Filore must be stronger than anyone’s.

Though, the mettle of the man suspected of being one of the three great leaders of the Demi-Human Alliance wasn’t so brittle as to confess under the weight of the obsession dwelling in that gaze. In any case—

“…I understand how bad the situation is.”

Letting out a small sigh, Rem straightened her back with determination, seemingly forcing a shift in her mood. She then looked around at Otto and the others in the room, one by one,

“Frankly speaking, I’m very worried about that person who isn’t here now, but Emilia-san at the castle and my sister are in danger, aren’t they? How on earth do we get them out… no, Felt-san too. How do we get them out?”

“With Reinhard and the Knight Commander on the other side, a frontal breakthrough would be impossible no matter how ya slice it. We’d be talkin’ ‘bout ‘Gaddgi Guaddzeadd’s mountain retreat.’ We need a plan, Otto-nii.”

Garfiel, bearing heavy burdens from the fight with Reinhard, agreed with Rem and turned expectant eyes toward Otto for a strategy.

The two were worried about Emilia, confined in the castle, and even more so about Ram’s safety, who had accompanied her as an attendant.

It was truly heart-wrenching to tell them this, but—

“—No, we will prioritize escaping the royal capital from now on. We don’t have the leeway to rescue Emilia-sama, Ram-san, or Felt-sama from the Royal Castle.”

““Wha—!?””

Their eyes wide with shock, Garfiel and Rem raised their voices simultaneously. Garfiel stood up, hand against the wall, and barked, “Otto-nii!”

“Escape… You mean we’re not gonna rescue ’em? What’re ya sayin’!”

“Exactly what I said. Currently, we lack both the means and the combat strength to rescue Emilia-sama and the others imprisoned in the castle. Any half-baked plan would be crushed by force, and we’d all just fall into enemy hands together. We can’t fight a losing battle like that.”

“A losing battle… But my sister is there, Emilia-san is…”

“I understand. But even if the enemy controls the Royal Castle, they can’t easily lay hands on a royal selection candidate. Even a compliant Reinhard-san wouldn’t overlook such a move. Their freedom will be restricted, but as long as they’re in the castle, the likelihood that Emilia-sama and the others won’t be directly harmed is high. That’s what I think.”

“B-but, still…”

Otto, striving to remain calm and keep emotion from his voice, was met with intense agitation from the visibly emotional Garfiel and Rem. But then, Old Man Rom raised a large hand and interjected, “May I?”

“I agree with the young lad. Even if they stay at the castle, Felt and the others will manage somehow. What’s scarier is our movements giving those bastards a pretext. They already have bad press from the mabeast incident. We’d best avoid anything more.”

“Even Old Man Rom agrees with Otto-san… Wilhelm-san, what about you?”

“—. I understand Rem-dono’s feelings, worrying for her master and her sister. However, challenging a fully prepared opponent while knowingly at a disadvantage is a foolish strategy… Infuriating as it is, Barga has a point.”

Slowly shaking his head, Wilhelm’s reply made Rem bite her lip.

Otto understood her regret all too painfully. Moreover, there was something Otto had deliberately not mentioned, to avoid further opposition from her and Garfiel.

Even if the ‘Sage Council’ had fallen into enemy hands, they couldn’t harm the royal selection candidates. —But that didn’t guarantee the safety of their attendants and aides.

And that Ram, having volunteered for the attendant role, had been prepared for that possibility from the start, which was why she hadn’t yielded the position to Rem or Petra.

“…Otto-nii, about leavin’ the capital… what about the boss and the others?”

“—. Joining up will be difficult. My Divine Protection is unreliable now. If I choose the wrong person to talk to carelessly, our internal situation will be an open book. We can’t take that risk.”

If only his ‘Divine Protection of Word Spirits’ were fully functional, he could have relied on numbers to search for Subaru and the others, send messages, or inform them of a rendezvous point.

With that option gone, finding Subaru and the others without any leads was impossible.

“Barga, I won’t press deeply. But surely, even you could find Crusch-sama or Subaru-dono…”

“You seem to think highly of a mere old man from the slums, but unfortunately, I can’t live up to those expectations. I’m not being mysterious or holding back. The fragile thread has already frayed. And I have no prospect of reconnecting it.”

“Useless when it counts… no, I’m no different.”

He reflexively started a biting remark, but Wilhelm hesitated, checking himself. The regret in Old Man Rom’s eyes as he glanced at him suggested his words weren’t a lie.

Indeed, no moves left to make. At this situation, Rem clutched the hem of her skirt tightly,

“I can believe in my sister and the others at the castle. I can believe that we here can work together. But as for that person… he’ll surely do something reckless. I know it.”

“Rem…”

“No, not just me. You know too, don’t you, Garf? Otto-san? That person, at times like this, absolutely tries to do more than he can handle. And because of that, he’ll surely end up terribly hurt again. And yet…”

We can’t be by his side. No one could say anything to Rem, whose voice was filled with heartfelt regret.

That’s surely because everyone here had been involved with Subaru in some way, been saved by Subaru’s actions just as Rem described, and had actually witnessed him getting hurt.

“…We’ll be placing a burden on Beatrice-chan.”

Now, Beatrice, the only one likely with Subaru, was the hope of the entire camp, serving as the safety net for him—just as Otto and the others thought that, “No,” Wilhelm stepped forward.

Standing before the downcast Rem, he gently placed a hand on her shoulder,

“Worrying is only natural. However, Subaru-dono is not with Beatrice-dono alone. Crusch-sama is with them.”

“—. But isn’t the prevailing rumor that the Duchess has lost her mind? In fact, didn’t she slip out of the mansion right under your nose?”

“Silence, Barga. What could you possibly know about that person?”

“——”

“As you say, I may have been deceived. But Crusch-sama must have had something in mind. I will not arbitrarily decide otherwise until I hear it from her own lips.”

It wasn’t merely a retort to a provocation; Wilhelm’s voice carried a firm core. With loyalty to his absent master dwelling in his blue eyes, he looked over Rem and everyone in the room.

“Even if her memories were taken, the righteousness of her heart remains undiminished. It’s not merely because of a debt regarding my wife. —I have decided, with my whole soul, to place that person on the throne.”

“Wilhelm-san…”

“That very Crusch-sama is there. Subaru-dono is a man of his own caliber. And Beatrice-dono, while she appears as an innocent child when with Subaru-dono, is, I’ve heard, a Great Spirit who has lived for many long years. She will surely overturn our concerns.”

The slowly spun words weren’t meant to persuade Rem, but rather, forcefully scrawled his wishes upon the world, as if willing it to be so.

It was a statement that even felt arrogant in a way, yet coming from the ‘Sword Devil’—who had once felled the ‘Sword Saint’ and achieved a marriage that defied fate—it carried the persuasive power to make one believe it. An uncanny power that made one think it just might happen.

And that—

“…Sounds like somethin’ the boss would say.”

“If that’s the case, it’s quite an honor to be compared so.”

When Garfiel voiced what Otto had also felt, Wilhelm nodded deeply. Bathed in the ‘Sword Devil’’s appeal up close, Rem let the tension drain from her shoulders.

Anxiety still lingered in her eyes and expression, but Rem bravely lifted her face,

“I’m sorry for losing my composure. You’re right. If Beatrice-chan and Karsten-san are reliable, then his one or two reckless acts… will it stop at just one or two?”

“Just when it seemed like you were convinced, isn’t it counterproductive to get stuck on that point…?”

“Three or four, Beatrice will handle ’em all! …Right now, believin’ that’s all we can do, yeah.”

Garfiel, still not completely free of his own worries, hid them and clicked his fangs, causing a faint smile to return to Rem’s lips. Otto felt the same. No one could completely dispel their anxieties and act in this situation. They might later regret this day if an undesired outcome came.

Even so, they had no choice but to choose, to overcome this moment’s hesitation.

“Then, once more—we will withdraw from the royal capital.”

Clearing his throat, Otto looked around at everyone and declared their policy again.

The Royal Castle had already fallen into enemy hands, and they themselves had become wanted fugitives targeted by the capital’s soldiers. If they moved carelessly, the ‘Sword Saint’ would be sent after them; if caught, they were in a dire situation where even their lives were at risk.

Leaving the royal capital in such a state would naturally be seen as turning tail and fleeing.

But he would dare to say it.

“This is by no means defeat. —This is a preparatory measure, to ensure that next time, we will strike back and win.”

“Heh, that’s the Otto-nii I know, a declaration brimmin’ with fightin’ spirit!”

“I completely disagree with that assessment, though!”

Among the nodding faces, Otto raised his voice in response to Garfiel, who rubbed his nose with a finger. Then, after nodding back at Rem, he turned his thoughts beyond the walls—towards the royal capital.

He didn’t know where they were. But he thought of Subaru and Beatrice, who, somewhere in this capital, were surely flailing in a dead end just like Otto and the others.

The anxiety was endless. Because, as Rem said, he would likely do something reckless.

—I don’t want him to. That was Otto’s true feeling.

“…Even so, you’ll do something reckless anyway, won’t you?”

Letting the walls absorb a murmur that would never reach, Otto pulled his hat down low over his eyes.

He knew all too well that the man named Natsuki Subaru would casually put his own life and heart on the scale if it meant meeting someone’s expectations or wishes. So, truthfully, he didn’t want to rely on him, and shouldn’t.

Counting on Natsuki Subaru as “the person who will do something about it,” just like everyone else did, felt, to Otto, the same as feeding on that kindness and weakness.

So, so at the very least—

So that his recklessness wouldn’t consume Natsuki Subaru and crush him.
Even from afar, to resist this irrationality and absurdity, they must do what they must—Otto thought, with a bitter feeling.

△▼△▼△▼△

“Achoo!”

“Did your body get cold? Are you alright, I wonder?”

“Nah, my nose just suddenly tickled. …Yeah, I do feel a chill, though.”

Having sneezed from the sudden sensation in his nose, Subaru relaxed his lips at Beatrice’s worried expression, but then sighed, unable to even maintain a wry smile.

That’s how shocking the story he’d heard from Russell had been—about the ‘Lust’ Capella’s secret maneuvering and the dire situation the Kingdom of Lugunica was in.

“Shape-shifters are always a troublesome bunch, it’s a cliché… but to this extent? It all makes sense now, how they’ve been harassing you and Russell-san for thirty years.”

“To be precise, it was the kingdom being harassed, and we were trying to stop it. Though, in our current disgraceful state, we’re in no position to talk big.”

“We understand that you were holding the line. But I don’t get it, I wonder. Why did that thirty-year tug-of-war suddenly tilt?”

“That’s… probably because of us, we think.”

Eyes downcast, the ‘White Sheep’ hung her head dejectedly and confessed.

Before the suspicious Subaru and Beatrice, her eyes filled with guilt and self-reproach,

“Because we left the ‘Beloved Children’ and joined up with the ‘Silver Foxes’ like this, there were several opportunities where their plans were foiled. So, we think Mama realized it too. —That the ‘Six Tongues’ had caught their trail.”

“Well, if traitors come from your own side, it’s natural to think that way… Or is it that? Capella’s the type to forget about something once it leaves her sight and never think of it again?”

“No, she’s rather the type to remember it forever.”

“I knew it! Consistent characterization. Truly, what a nasty piece of work, that one…”

His recognition of Capella’s twisted personality reinforced, Subaru spat out the words. As if to supplement the exchange with the ‘White Sheep,’ Russell raised another finger,

“As she says, the enemy likely knew our hand had become stronger. When the Witch’s Cult attacked Pristella all at once, the fact that ‘Lust,’ who hadn’t appeared openly before, brazenly showed herself was likely intended to make us aware of her existence.”

“Aware of her existence? What’s the point of doing that…”

“—The ‘Six Tongues’ have already dealt with seven Capella Emerada Lugunicas.”

“Huh?”

“It’s a fact. If there’s an eyewitness report matching the physical characteristics of the ‘Lust’ confirmed at Pristella, we’re forced to move to verify it. And naturally, during that time, other schemes using the ‘Beloved Children’ are deployed. This recent affair is my fault for being spread too thin dealing with all of that.”

“…Needless to say, I suppose, but those seven were fakes, I wonder?”

“Yes. It goes without saying.”

Russell explained flatly and without emotion that they’d been outplayed, but Subaru felt if he were in Russell’s shoes, there was no way he could avoid flying into a rage.

Even that atrocity in Pristella that birthed so many tragedies was nothing more than a preparatory measure for Capella to later toy with the ‘Six Tongues’ and seize control of the kingdom?

After ruining and twisting the lives of so many people.

“—Ngh.”

“…Subaru, I understand how you feel, but calm down, I wonder. You’re only hurting yourself, I suppose.”

“Eh? Ah, sorry. I just…”

Clenching his teeth and hanging his head, Subaru felt Beatrice, sitting in his lap, press her back against his chest. She gently touched his right arm—the arm he’d unconsciously been digging his nails into.

Looking, the handkerchief wrapped around the burn he’d received at the McMahon residence had been removed and replaced with a clean bandage. Over that bandage, he’d been about to dig his left nails in again. As he was about to apologize for the unconscious act, he suddenly realized something. —The pain from the burn was completely gone.

“Was this you, Russell-san… no, ‘White Sheep’-chan?”

“Eh? Ah, no, it wasn’t me. That was ‘Blue Snake’-san…”

“‘Blue Snake’?”

“—One of my subordinates. A skilled healer. It was presumptuous, but we treated your injuries while you were asleep. If there’s no discomfort…”

“Ah, yeah, none at all. So little it’s actually disconcerting.”

He lightly rotated his wrist and swung his arm; it felt like perfect condition.

Even accounting for his mana shortage, Beatrice’s power had only managed first aid for that burn. For it to be healed this well meant Russell’s subordinates were quite a talented bunch.

If there were any injuries, Crusch and Rachins would likely have been healed too.

Though—

“—Old man.”

Hand to his mouth, lost in thought, Rachins was in no state to care about that now.

If it was true that Capella’s clutches had reached the Royal Castle and that high-ranking nobles, starting with the ‘Sage Council,’ had been replaced, then Rachins’ father was likely among them.

Despite his verbal defiance towards his father, the surprising intellect Rachins occasionally showed seemed to have a core rooted in his background. And it was likely that core was not unrelated to the father in question.

“Rachins, you alright?”

“…I just got stripped half-naked. What’s the big deal?”

“—. I see. Got it.”

Though he probably understood the intent of the question, Subaru respected Rachins’ answer.

It was only natural to be worried. And at this very moment, Subaru had no means to resolve it, nor did Rachins seem to want to talk about what was in his heart.

Worrying about one’s family was a heavy feeling. Experience taught him that talking didn’t necessarily make it easier, so he couldn’t force it. Of course, he intended to listen if Rachins wanted to talk, but—

“—Oi oi, is that dreary, tedious talk finally over?”

Barging in and shattering that delicate atmosphere, the door to the infirmary was violently thrown open.

Stomping in with rude footsteps was a dangerous figure in black clothing and a jester’s mask—the ‘Black Dog,’ carrying a large bag in one hand.

Since Subaru and the others were safe, he’d naturally assumed the same for him, but…

“You really know how to kill a conversation… We were in the middle of something important.”

“Oh, terribly sorry for your busy life. With you scrambling to survive, you’d probably say there’s not a single moment you aren’t discussing something important!”

“‘Black Dog,’ stop it! Natsuki Subaru-san is our benefactor!”

“Ooh, acting all high and mighty. A wicked woman skilled at making others wag their tails, yet not satisfied wagging your own—it’s not your head but your rear that hangs low, kahahaha!”

“Ugh—!”

The ‘White Sheep,’ who had scolded the ‘Black Dog’’s rude attitude, turned red at his counterattack.

It seemed that despite both having left the ‘Beloved Children’ together, these two didn’t share the deeply trusting relationship like Subaru and Beatrice.

Of course, Subaru wanted to side with the ‘White Sheep’—

“You’re talking too much, ‘Black Dog.’ Keep it brief.”

“Tch. The preparations to move are almost done, so you lot better get ready too, that’s all!”

“Woof woof, I wonder!”
“Whoa, what the—!?”

Speaking roughly, the ‘Black Dog’ suddenly hurled the large bag he was holding at Subaru and the others. Subaru and Beatrice instinctively recoiled as it fell at their feet onto the bed.

As the ‘White Sheep’ hastily opened the bag, inside were—

“—Robes with a ‘Perception Obstruction’ effect. Acquired through Roswaal. There are ones for Natsuki-dono, Beatrice-dono, and also Crusch-sama. Ah, and a spare for Rachins-dono as well.”

“Thanks for treating me like an afterthought!”

